\section*{Talagrand discrete convexity conjecture}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Talagrand discrete convexity}: The paper claims a universal number of family members whose unions leave only a small containment-cover obstruction, provided the original Bernoulli measure is sufficiently close to one. The cover cost is bounded at the original density.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Talagrand discrete convexity}: A biased orthogonal expansion produces nonnegative subset weights with a global budget. A signed many-row identity turns failure of a union cover into a large higher-power sum of those weights. Large weights give cheap generators directly, while weighted-degree selection and random unions cover the remaining classes; a final density transfer gives the universal union count.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Talagrand discrete convexity}: The manuscript’s result supplies k=2\textasciicircum{}75, every finite nonempty ground set, every p in (0,1), arbitrary D of measure>=1-1/k, and a cover of E\_k(D) with sum p\textasciicircum{}|I|<=1/2. Tuple members may repeat and D need not be increasing. Relabeling [N] gives arbitrary X; the empty-ground-set case has empty E\_k(D). The explicit k strengthens the existential statement.

This page records partial support. Complete resolution of the catalogue question is not established by this record.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 175 describes Lean coverage for the integral/fractional threshold comparison and the discrete-convexity statement; graph decomposition is a later separate consequence.

\textit{Talagrand discrete convexity}: The manuscript links \texttt{TalagrandDiscreteConvexity.lean} (declaration \texttt{OAI.TalagrandDiscreteConvexity.talagrand\_discrete\_convexity}), \texttt{TalagrandExpectationThreshold.lean} (declaration \texttt{OAI.TalagrandThreshold.talagrand\_expectation\_threshold\_equivalence}).

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

